|       \|/    C hemicals     |  PEQ 2026-27 -- Green ammonia, 1200 t/day     |
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
//  CATALYST BED 1 of 3 -- an ADIABATIC plug-flow bed on the Dyson & Simon
//  (1968) rate law for an IRON catalyst, WRITTEN in the case's
//  constant/reactions (`type expression`, every constant with its unit).
//  Its inlet temperature is what the FEHE delivers; its outlet temperature and
//  conversion are RESULTS.
//
//  THE VOLUME IS SOLVED AGAINST A STATED CRITERION.  The first bed is the
//  hottest: an adiabatic bed fed at ~381 C heats itself as it converts, and
//  run to a 5 K approach it would leave above the 520 C the base data give as
//  the catalyst's limit.  So bed 1 is sized to LEAVE AT 515 C (788.15 K), 5 K
//  under that limit.  The volume is `$V_Bed1`, declared in the PLANT's
//  `variables {}` (system/flowsheetDict) and SOLVED by the DesignSpec in
//  system/outerDict (Bed1.T_out = 788.15 K, simultaneously with beds 2 and
//  3).  A unit folder with no `variables {}` of its own resolves its
//  `$references` against the plant's -- until DEV.md 4c C66 it did not, and
//  this number was found by hand.  The run prints `T_out` and `approach_K`:
//  read both.
//
//  THE BED HAS A CROSS-SECTION, AND THE GAS LOSES PRESSURE CROSSING IT
//  (DEV.md 4c C66, C69).  The three beds sit in ONE pressure shell (the
//  CONVERTER sector; system/postDict's `converterShell`), so they share ONE
//  diameter, the shell's: `diameter $D_converter;`, a plant variable read by
//  every bed -- in the FLOWSHEET, because a bed needs its cross-section
//  DURING the solve (its Ergun drop is integrated along its length), and the
//  sizing pass runs after it.  Each bed's length is then its volume over that
//  cross-section, L = V_R / (pi D^2 / 4).  The DIAMETER IS SOLVED: the
//  DesignSpec in system/outerDict widens or narrows it until the LARGEST of
//  the three drops over allowable is exactly 1, which makes it the SMALLEST
//  diameter at which every bed is within its allowable (each drop falls
//  monotonically as the converter widens).  No bed is named in advance; the
//  run names the one that binds (bed 3, the largest volume and so the
//  longest at one diameter).  The converterShell sizer then brackets the
//  same smallest diameter on the solved beds and says whether the
//  converter's sits at it.
//  Until C69 each bed took its OWN slenderness, L/D 1.5 (the paragraph below
//  says why 1.5); three diameters cannot share one shell, and a one-shell
//  converter built around the widest of them cost MORE than three vessels.
//  Every number in the three lines below is AUTHOR-SET, none is a vendor's:
//    * particleDiameter 8 mm -- the middle of the 6-10 mm window Dyson &
//      Simon's Eq 39 was computed for, taken by Ergun as an EQUIVALENT-SPHERE
//      diameter, so for irregular granules the drop below is a LOWER estimate;
//    * voidFraction 0.40 -- a random-packing value; the drop goes as
//      (1-eps)/eps^3, so 0.36 instead would raise it by about half;
//    * allowable 1 bar per bed -- a stated design budget with no source
//      behind it; the run announces any bed above it.
//  WHY NOT A SLENDER CONVERTER (the history, C66: at each bed's own L/D the
//  case first carried the base case's converter ratio, 6).  Measured: at
//  L/D 6 the first pass of the loop lost 35, 24 and 16 bar across the three
//  beds, and the recycle did not converge (a later
//  iterate consumed a bed's whole inlet pressure, refused by name).  The
//  scaling below puts the converged L/D 6 drops near 11, 5 and 3 bar --
//  three-quarters of the 20 bar between the loop and the separator, on a
//  budget of 1 bar each.  At a fixed volume both
//  the superficial velocity and the length go as (L/D)^(2/3), so the drop
//  goes as (L/D)^2: at 1.5 the three beds lost 0.67, 0.31 and 0.17 bar.  A
//  squat bed is the axial-flow answer; a bed the gas crosses RADIALLY
//  (through its thickness, not its length) is the other, and this `pfr` does
//  not model one.

type      pfr;
operation
{
    V_R          $V_Bed1;       // catalyst bed -- SOLVED by system/outerDict; see above
    nSteps       400;           // RK4 axial steps
    thermalMode  adiabatic;     // T is a RESULT: the bed heats itself
    diameter     $D_converter;  // the CONVERTER's one diameter -- SOLVED; see above
    catalyst     { particleDiameter 8 mm; }                        // AUTHOR-SET, see above
    pressureDrop { model Ergun;  voidFraction 0.40;  allowable 1 bar; }   // AUTHOR-SET, see above
}
reactions ( ammoniaSynthesis );
boundary  { inlets ( Feed );  outlets ( Out ); }
